### Example 3

```text
S = "abab"
K = 2
```

Valid unique substrings:

```text
ab
ba
aba
bab
abab
```

Answer:

```text
5
```

---

# What concept is involved?

Many students immediately think:

```text
Sliding Window
```

But Sliding Window only counts:

```text
number of substrings
```

It does NOT handle:

```text
distinct substrings
```

because:

```text
abab
```

contains `"ab"` multiple times.

We must count it only once.

---

# Brute Force Idea

Generate every substring:

```java
for(i)
   for(j)
```

Check:

```java
distinct characters == K
```

Store in:

```java
HashSet<String>
```

Finally:

```java
set.size()
```

---

### Code

```java
HashSet<String> set = new HashSet<>();

for(int i=0;i<n;i++) {

    int[] freq = new int[26];
    int distinct = 0;

    for(int j=i;j<n;j++) {

        int idx = s.charAt(j)-'a';

        if(freq[idx]==0)
            distinct++;

        freq[idx]++;

        if(distinct==K)
            set.add(s.substring(i,j+1));

        if(distinct>K)
            break;
    }
}

System.out.println(set.size());
```

---

## Complexity

```text
O(n²)
```

This works for:

```text
n ≤ 1000
```

but NOT for:

```text
n = 100000
```

---

# If the constraint 10^5 is genuine

Then the intended solution involves:

```text
Suffix Automaton
or
Suffix Array + LCP
```

combined with character-frequency tracking.

Reason:

```text
Distinct Substrings
```

is usually a giveaway for:

```text
Suffix Automaton
Suffix Array
Trie of suffixes
Rolling Hash
```

rather than pure Sliding Window.

---

# Interview / Contest Hint

Whenever you see:

```text
Count substrings
```

→ Sliding Window may work.

Whenever you see:

```text
Count DISTINCT substrings
```

→ Think:

```text
HashSet (small constraints)
Suffix Automaton / Suffix Array (large constraints)
```

That's the key pattern this problem is testing.
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        String s = sc.next();
        int k = sc.nextInt();

        HashSet<String> set = new HashSet<>();

        int n = s.length();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            int[] freq = new int[26];
            int distinct = 0;

            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {

                int idx = s.charAt(j) - 'a';

                if (freq[idx] == 0)
                    distinct++;

                freq[idx]++;

                if (distinct == k) {
                    set.add(s.substring(i, j + 1));
                }

                // Once distinct characters exceed K,
                // extending the substring can never bring it back to K
                if (distinct > k)
                    break;
            }
        }

        System.out.println(set.size());
    }
}